**OPEN:** I found no verified polynomial-time reduction for Q1(a), (b), or (c), including an almost-all reduction for the specified non-CM forms. This is a literature-search conclusion, not an impossibility theorem.

**MODEL:** A rigorous result available here is a constant-information bound for independent Frobenius congruences: \(K\) curves supply **less than \(1.187K\) bits** about the hidden determinant residues, even allowing arbitrarily many distinct odd auxiliary primes. Its restrictions are essential; it does not bound algorithms using the exact integer coefficients.

## 1. Q1 — Reductions and proposed attacks

**KNOWN.** Eric Bach and Denis Charles, “The hardness of computing an eigenform,” *Contemporary Mathematics* **463** (2008), 9–15, arXiv v2 Theorem 2.2 and Remark 2.5, give a density-one reduction, including balanced moduli. Their algorithm queries **both \(N\) and \(N^2\)**. An algorithm callable at arbitrary indices is therefore stronger than the single supplied value in this question. [Paper](https://arxiv.org/pdf/0708.1192)

**PROVED — the missing equation.** For trivial character, put \(w=k-1\), \(u=a_f(p)\), \(v=a_f(q)\), \(A=a_f(N)\), and \(B=a_f(N^2)\). Then
\[
A=uv,\qquad
B=A^2+N^w-u^2q^w-v^2p^w.
\]
Consequently \(u^2/p^w\) and \(v^2/q^w\) are roots of
\[
Z^2-\frac{A^2+N^w-B}{N^w}Z+\frac{A^2}{N^w}=0.
\]
Their reduced denominators reveal factors of \(N\). For nonzero traces and sufficiently large primes, those denominators are nontrivial: \(w\) is odd, and \(p^w\mid u^2\) would imply \(p^{w+1}\mid u^2\), contradicting \(u^2\le4p^w\). The analogous cross term is absent from \(A\) alone.

**PROVED — information counting does not establish hardness.** Writing \(n=\log_2N\),
\[
|A|\le4N^{(k-1)/2}.
\]
Thus a weight-2 datum has at most \(n/2+O(1)\) bits, comparable with the size of one factor; \(\tau(N)\) has at most \(11n/2+O(1)\) bits. Neither count proves insufficient information. These bounds follow from the prime coefficient bound in Edixhoven–Couveignes, *Computational Aspects of Modular Forms and Galois Representations*, Annals of Mathematics Studies **176** (2011), Theorem 2.4.18. [Book](https://arxiv.org/pdf/math/0605244)

**PROVED — what the elementary equations leave free.** For \(K\) coefficients, the equations
\[
pq=N,\qquad u_iv_i=A_i\quad(1\le i\le K)
\]
have \(K+1\) continuous degrees of freedom: choose \(p,u_1,\ldots,u_K\), then set \(q=N/p\), \(v_i=A_i/u_i\). Strict Hasse inequalities preserve an open region. This concerns these equations only, not the additional arithmetic constraints of actual Fourier coefficients.

**KNOWN / OPEN — Coppersmith.** Don Coppersmith, “Small solutions to polynomial equations, and low exponent RSA vulnerabilities,” *Journal of Cryptology* **10** (1997), 233–260, §11, Theorems 4–5, factors balanced \(N\) from roughly half of one factor’s bits. Hasse bounds provide neither such bits nor a known approximation to \(p\) within \(O(N^{1/4})\). Applying a lattice to \(uv=A\) addresses splitting \(A\); it supplies no equation coupling that split to \(p\). No successful general multivariate attack was verified. [Paper](https://link.springer.com/content/pdf/10.1007/s001459900030.pdf)

**PROVED / OPEN — factoring the coefficient.** For \(A\ne0\), its factorization permits enumeration of at most \(2d(|A|)\) ordered signed trace pairs. This is not a worst-case polynomial bound, and recovering the correct traces still does not furnish a verified general factoring reduction. For weight 2, \(A\) has approximately half as many bits as \(N\), but factoring it remains a non-polynomial step. I found neither a proved implementation of Q1(c) after factoring all \(A_i\), nor a proved way around that step.

**PROVED — dependent curves add nothing.** Quadratic twists satisfy
\[
a_{E^{(d)}}(N)=\left(\frac dN\right)a_E(N).
\]
This follows by changing the prime character sums and multiplying. Hence arbitrarily many such twists can be simulated from one coefficient using Jacobi symbols. Isogenous curves likewise have identical good-prime traces.

**PROVED — exceptional congruences can help.** Rational \(m\)-torsion gives \(a_r\equiv r+1\pmod m\), hence
\[
A\equiv N+1+p+q\pmod m.
\]
For 11a1, \(m=5\): on its usual model, successive multiples of \((5,5)\) are
\[
(16,-61),(16,60),(5,-6),O.
\]
Reduction at good primes preserves this order away from 5. This yields \(p+q\bmod5\), not a growing modulus.

**OPEN.** Adding \(\tau(N)\), unrelated curves, or a polynomial-length prefix of a fixed enumeration has no verified general reduction. Assuming independence for such growing families is **HEURISTIC**, not a consequence of fixed-field Chebotarev.

## 2. Q2 — Native computation

Here \(\widetilde O\) suppresses logarithmic factors; these are upper bounds for specified implementations, not lower bounds.

| Route | Status and cost |
|---|---|
| Modular symbols / Merel matrices | **KNOWN:** \(O(\sigma_1(N)\log N)\) arithmetic operations; balanced semiprimes give \(\widetilde O(N)\). |
| Complete Jacobi sum | **PROVED:** \(N\) Jacobi evaluations, hence \(\widetilde O(N)\) bit operations and polynomial-logarithmic space. |
| Fixed quaternary theta coefficient | **PROVED:** meet-in-the-middle gives \(\widetilde O(N)\) time and \(O(N)\) stored entries. |
| Binary-theta convolution | **PROVED:** sieve binary representation counts and evaluate the length-\(N\) convolution in \(\widetilde O(N)\) time. |
| Standard \(q\)-expansions | **KNOWN:** approximately linear precision-dependent work for the examples; no verified isolated-index shortcut here. |
| Trace formulas | **KNOWN:** \(O(\sqrt N)\) class-number terms, plus divisor terms; this is not by itself a native sublinear algorithm. |

The modular-symbol bound is stated explicitly in William Stein, *Modular Forms: A Computational Approach*, GSM **79**, AMS (2007), §8.3.3, using Merel’s matrix families. [Complexity discussion](https://www.wstein.org/books/modform/modform/modular_symbols.html#remarks-on-complexity)

**PROVED — counting details.** A fixed rational diagonalization reduces a positive quaternary form to diagonal coordinates with finitely many congruence conditions. Enumerate the \(O(N)\) possibilities for each coordinate pair and match their values and residue conditions.

For the stated binary form \(B\), its class-number-one norm interpretation gives, for \(m>0\),
\[
r_B(m)=2\sum_{d\mid m}\chi_{-11}(d).
\]
Sieving these sums costs \(O(N\log N)\); the final convolution costs \(O(N)\). Expanding it into divisor variables does not automatically shorten the computation: a direct double-hyperbola treatment still has approximately \(N\) divisor pairs.

**KNOWN — a relevant additional paper.** Denis Xavier Charles, “Computing the Ramanujan tau function,” *Ramanujan Journal* **11** (2006), 221–224, §2, gives \(O(n^{3/4+\varepsilon})\) deterministic computation and \(O(n^{1/2+\varepsilon})\) randomized computation under GRH. However, §2 explicitly begins by factoring \(n\), then computes prime-index coefficients. These are therefore not native bounds under the question’s restriction. [Published article](https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s11139-006-6509-y), [author-text search result](https://pages.cs.wisc.edu/~cdx/CompTau-rev.pdf)

**PROVED — trace-formula caution.** For nonsquare \(N=pq\), the level-one divisor term includes
\[
\frac12\sum_{dd'=N}\min(d,d')^{k-1}=1+p^{k-1}.
\]
Replacing its direct evaluation by class-number identities can merely conceal a factoring step. Any claimed native improvement must specify how it handles both this term and the class-number subroutines.

**OPEN / UNVERIFIED literature completeness.** I found no verified published \(N^{1-\delta}\) native bound for the specified non-CM cusp coefficients. This does not exclude one.

**OPEN — analytic routes.** Fourier extraction, theta transformations, and trace formulas provide reorganizations, but I verified no exact sublinear algorithm under the native restriction. Cancellation or faster exact shifted-convolution evaluation could conceivably improve the power of \(N\). No construction approaching \(L_N[1/3]\) emerged, and no theorem rules one out.

## 3. Q3 — Two explicit model barriers

### A. A constant-bit bound

**MODEL — definition.** For each auxiliary odd prime \(\ell\), choose a hidden uniform \(D_\ell\in\mathbf F_\ell^\times\), and expose \(C_\ell\in\mathbf F_\ell^\times\). Independently for each of \(K\) curves, sample uniform matrices \(g_i,h_i\) with determinants \(D_\ell,C_\ell/D_\ell\). Observe
\[
A_{i,\ell}=\operatorname{tr}(g_i)\operatorname{tr}(h_i).
\]
Different auxiliary primes are independent. Unlimited postprocessing is allowed.

This model exposes residue data, not an actual integer \(N\), exact bounded traces, elliptic-curve group operations, or torsion-algebra computations.

**MODEL — theorem and proof.** For every finite set \(S\) of odd primes,
\[
I\bigl((D_\ell)_{\ell\in S};(A_{i,\ell})_{i,\ell}\mid(C_\ell)_{\ell\in S}\bigr)
<1.187K\quad\text{bits}.
\]

For determinant \(d\), elementary matrix counting gives
\[
P_d(t)=\frac{\ell+\chi(t^2-4d)}{\ell^2-1}.
\]
Indeed, write a matrix as \(\begin{pmatrix}a&b\\c&t-a\end{pmatrix}\) and count solutions of \(bc=a(t-a)-d\).

Against the uniform distribution \(U\) on \(\mathbf F_\ell\),
\[
|\ell P_d(t)-1|\le(\ell-1)^{-1},
\qquad
\chi^2(P_d\Vert U)\le(\ell-1)^{-2}.
\]
Two ordered traces therefore convey at most
\[
2\log_2\!\left(1+(\ell-1)^{-2}\right)
\]
bits about \(d\): bound relative entropy against the fixed uniform reference distribution. Taking their product cannot increase information. Conditional independence and entropy subadditivity extend the bound to \(K\) curves. Finally,
\[
\sum_{\ell\ {\rm odd\ prime}}
2\log_2(1+(\ell-1)^{-2})
\le\frac{2}{\ln2}\sum_{j\ge1}(2j)^{-2}
=\frac{\pi^2}{12\ln2}<1.187.
\]
This proves the assertion.

### B. Scalar symmetry, including arbitrarily many curves

**MODEL — theorem and proof.** Suppose a joint image \(G\subseteq\prod_i\mathrm{GL}_2(\mathbf F_\ell)\) contains every common scalar \((\lambda I)_i\). Uniform pairs in \(G^2\) are invariant under
\[
(g,h)\longmapsto(\lambda g,\lambda^{-1}h).
\]
Every trace product and determinant product is preserved, while the first determinant changes by \(\lambda^2\). Thus the posterior determinant is uniform within square classes, whatever \(K\) is. Swapping \(g,h\) shows that when \(C\) is nonsquare, even those two classes are indistinguishable.

**MODEL — mixed weights.** The same proof works in a weighted joint image containing scalars \((\lambda^{k_i-1}I)_i\). Therefore adding weight 12 to weight 2 does not automatically remove this local symmetry.

**PROVED — limits.** Neither result is an integer-factoring barrier. Actual traces satisfy simultaneous integrality and size constraints discarded by the models. For actual integers, \(H(p\mid N)=0\); ordinary Shannon information conditional on \(N\) cannot establish computational hardness.

Algorithms using the group law on \(E(\mathbf Z/N)\), gcds, Jacobi symbols of constructed values, or \((\mathbf Z/N)[x]/(\psi_\ell)\) are **outside these barriers**. No impossibility result covering all those operations is proved here.

**PROVED — compatibility with the known reductions.**

- Bach–Charles uses exact rational denominators. Even its additional symmetric-square observation retains a local scalar symmetry; exact integer structure defeats that local ambiguity.
- The CM pair uses a norm identity. Write \(p=a^2+b^2\), \(q=c^2+d^2\), with \(a,c\) odd. If \(D\) is nonsquare at both primes, the two supplied products give \(X=\pm ac\), \(Y=\pm bd\). Then \(a^2d^2,b^2c^2\) are roots of
  \[
  Z^2-(N-X^2-Y^2)Z+X^2Y^2.
  \]
  A gcd with \(X^2+Z\) yields a factor. If \(D\) is nonsquare at exactly one prime, \(\gcd(N,X^2+Y^2)\) suffices.

The classical trace formulas used here are in Kenneth Ireland and Michael Rosen, *A Classical Introduction to Modern Number Theory*, second edition, GTM **84**, Springer (1990), Chapter 18, §4. [Text](https://gaetan.chenevier.perso.math.cnrs.fr/GT/ireland_rosen.pdf)

## 4. Q4 — Additional literature and exact checks

**KNOWN.** A particularly relevant additional statement is Peter Bruin, “Computing coefficients of modular forms,” *Publications mathématiques de Besançon* (2011), 19–36, **Theorem 1.1 and Remark 1.3**. The theorem takes the index in factored form; the remark explains why removing that requirement would imply polynomial-time factoring, using an Eisenstein series. It does not prove hardness from one non-CM cusp coefficient. [Published source](https://pmb.centre-mersenne.org/articles/10.5802/pmb.a-133/)

**KNOWN.** Steven D. Galbraith and James F. McKee, “Pairings on elliptic curves over finite commutative rings,” *Cryptography and Coding*, LNCS **3796** (2005), 392–409, §§2–3, discusses known point-count inputs and extension rings. Its input is different from \(a_pa_q\). [Paper](https://www.math.auckland.ac.nz/~sgal018/pair-mod-n.pdf)

**OPEN / UNVERIFIED completeness.** Beyond the works named in the question and those above, I found no verified positive or negative theorem resolving the single-coefficient problem.

**PROVED — finite exact checks.** The [scratch harness]( /Volumes/git/factoring-connection/codex/scratch/cuspcoef_1/phase30_blind_check.py) runs with
```sh
/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/.venv/bin/python phase30_blind_check.py
```

It gives:

- \(N=101\cdot113=11413\), 37a1: \((a_p,a_q,A,B)=(3,-18,-54,-19412)\); the complete Jacobi sum is \(-54\), and the quadratic above yields \(9/101,324/113\).
- CM example \(N=101\cdot109=11009\): products \(-12,400\); cross squares \(900,100\); recovered factors \(101,109\).
- Exhaustion of all \(2016\) matrices in \(\mathrm{GL}_2(\mathbf F_7)\) verifies the scalar symmetry exactly.

The checks took approximately \(0.04\) seconds and 38 MiB peak resident memory. Known primes were harness inputs; these checks do not constitute a non-CM factoring algorithm. All processes exited.